# Operator-managed GitHub App registration, the supported way a tenant connects
# a GitHub source: the App is registered once for the deployment, each tenant
# installs it on the repositories it picks, and the host mints a short-lived
# installation token narrowed to one repository for every fetch.
#
# The numeric id GitHub assigns the App. It is not a secret — it is visible in
# the App's own install URL — so it is declared here rather than beside the
# signing key. Empty means no App is registered for this deployment, and every
# source keeps authenticating with its own stored fine-grained PAT.
GITHUB_APP_ID=
# The App's URL slug, the last segment of its public page
# (https://github.com/apps/<slug>). It is what the install link is built from,
# so tenant-driven App onboarding stays off until it is set; sources can still
# be connected with a repository-scoped fine-grained PAT. Not a secret.
GITHUB_APP_SLUG=
# The App's OAuth client id. Tenant onboarding trades the authorization code
# GitHub appends to the setup redirect for a user-to-server token, and that is
# what proves the person returning from the install can actually reach the
# installation they present — an installation id is a small integer arriving
# from a browser, so it authorizes nothing by itself. Requires "Request user
# authorization (OAuth) during installation" to be enabled on the App. Unset
# leaves App onboarding off; sources can still be connected with a
# repository-scoped fine-grained PAT. Not a secret.
GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=
# Whether the per-source push receiver is mounted: the endpoint a source
# connected with its own fine-grained PAT and its own webhook secret points its
# repository webhook at, /v1/datasource/github/webhook/<source id> on this
# deployment's public origin. It is separate from the App: an App-backed
# source's pushes arrive at the App's single webhook URL, which is mounted
# whenever an App and its webhook secret are registered. Off unless set to
# true, so a deployment that connects no PAT source exposes no such endpoint.
DATASOURCE_GITHUB_WEBHOOK_ENABLED=false
#
# One further setting has no variable here because GitHub holds it, not this
# deployment: the App's **Setup URL**, configured on the App registration
# itself. After a tenant installs the App, GitHub sends the browser to that URL
# with `installation_id`, `setup_action`, the `state` this host minted and the
# `code` the client id above is traded against, and the datasources page
# redeems them to finish the connection. Point it at the page that mounts the
# datasources panel — `https://<host>/admin/datasources` in the portal — and
# enable "Redirect on update" so that adding repositories to an existing
# installation returns the same way a first install does.
#
# Nothing here can verify it. With the id, slug and client id set but the
# Setup URL pointed elsewhere (or unset, which leaves the tenant on GitHub),
# the install succeeds, the setup state is never redeemed, it lapses after its
# short TTL, and the tenant is returned no source and no error. Check this
# first when App onboarding appears to do nothing.
